 { In view of the weak e-pawns and Black's ideally placed rook on e2, White seems to be a long way from victory. However... } 1. { -KEY- } Kh4 $1 { A key move, sacrificing any of the pawns in order to activate the king. } Rxe4+ ( { After } 1... Rxh2 $6 2. Kg5 Rg2 3. Kf6 { White's task is even easier. } ) 
2. Kg5 Rxe5+ 3. Kxg6 Re2 4. Rf7+ Ke8 ( { If } 4... Kg8 { then } 5. Rg7 Kf8 6. h4 $1 { and wins - Timman, since the h5-pawn won't survive for long; e.g. } Re3 7. Kh6 Re6 8. Rg6 Re5 9. Rg5 ) 
5. h3 $1 ( { Not } 5. h4 $2 Re3 { followed by 6...Rxg3, and Black draws comfortably thanks to the 'bad' rook's pawn. After the text, White will keep a knight's pawn instead. } ) 
Rg2 ( { Or } 5... Re3 6. g4 Rxh3 7. g5 h4 8. Rf4 { and wins - Timman. In fact we will soon reach the same position in the game. } ) 
6. Rf3 h4 7. g4 Rg3 8. Rf4 $1 Rxh3 9. g5 Ke7 { Thanks to the cut-off enemy king, White is easily winning. } 10. Kg7 ( { Preparing to push away Black's king by means of a vertical check. Instead } 10. Kh7 $2 { fails after } Rg3 11. g6 h3 12. g7 h2 13. Rh4 Kf7 $1 { etc. } ) 
Ke6 ( { If } 10... Rg3 { in similar fashion to the previous note, then after } 11. g6 h3 { White wins a crucial tempo by } 12. Re4 $1 Kd7 13. Kh7 h2 14. Rh4 { when Black can't stop the g-pawn and save the h-pawn at the same time. } ) 
11. g6 Ke5 12. Rf1 Rg3 13. Kf7 
 1-0

