 { In this delicate position, White correctly opted for active means. } 1. { -KEY- } f4 $1 { Preparing h4-h5 and f4-f5 in breakthrough fashion, so that the f7-pawn can be captured without entering a rook ending. } ( { Instead, after } 1. Rf8 $6 Rxc3 2. Nxf7 Bxf7 3. Rxf7 Kg8 4. Rg7 ( { or } 4. Rd7 Rd3 5. Rxd5 Kf7 { and Black holds; } ) 
Kf8 5. Rxg6 Rd3 { White is hardly any better - Carlsen. } ) 
 ( 1. Nc6 { heading for d8 is a different idea, when } Rxc3 2. Nd8 Rb3 $1 3. Ra8 Rb6 { just saves Black, as noticed by UltraSuperBrainPower. } ) 
Rxc3 ( { The restrictive } 1... h5 $2 { fails to } 2. Nf3  { and } 3. Ng5 ) 
2. h5 $1 gxh5 ( { a) After } 2... g5 $2 3. f5 Bxf5 4. Nxf7 { Black is mated. } ) 
 ( { b) While } 2... Rb3 3. hxg6 fxg6 4. Re8 Rb6 5. Re7 Kg8 6. Rg7 $1 Kf8 7. Nxg6 Ke8 8. Ne5 { followed by f4-f5, also wins for White - Carlsen. } ) 
3. Rf8 { With the inevitable threat of f4-f5. } Ra3 4. f5 Bxf5 5. Rxf7+ Kg8 6. Rg7+ Kf8 7. Rb7 Ra8 { At first sight it is far from clear how White can make progress, but Carlsen finds the key to the position. } 8. Kg3 $1 { Active king. Once the bishop is chased away from f5, the knight will gain access to either g6 or d7, and mate will be in the air. } Rd8 ( { Aronian enables an exchange sacrifice on d7 , seeing that } 8... c3 9. Kf4 c2 10. Kxf5 $1 c1=Q $11 11. Ng6 { leads to mate after } Ke8 ( { or } 11... Kg8 12. Rg7 ) 
12. f7 Kd8 13. f8=Q { - Carlsen. } ) 
9. Kf4 Be4 10. g3 { Avoiding any scenarios of a theoretical draw. } ( { Another path to victory was } 10. Nf7 { , followed by Nxh6 and Rg7-g8 in computer style - Carlsen. } ) 
c3 11. Rf7+ Kg8 12. Rg7+ Kf8 13. Nd7+ Rxd7 14. Rxd7 ( { In view of his desperate position after } 14. Rxd7 c2 15. Rc7 { Black resigned. } ) 

 1-0

