 { White has an extra pawn, but Black is very active. Dzagnidze found the only practical chance. } 1. { -KEY- } Re8+ Kf6 2. e5+ $1 dxe5 $6 ( { The unexpected } 2... Kf5 $1 { was called for, when White would have to settle for a draw; e.g. } 3. a6 ( { the point is that } 2... dxe5 2. exd6 $4 { fails to } Re1 ) 
Ra3 4. Kd2 Rxa6 5. exd6 Rxd6 6. Kc3 ) 
3. Ke4 { By this point the kings have definitely changed roles. } Rxa5 { It is hard to believe, but Black is now lost despite the extra pawn! } ( { In any case, there was no good alternative. For instance, after } 3... Rd1 $5 4. a6 Rd4 5. Ke3 Rd7 6. Ra8 $1 { followed by a6-a7 White soon wins by sacrificing the rook with check on the 8th rank, after which the pawn queens, now that Black's rook isn't ideally placed behind the passed pawn. Trying to prevent this scenario by } Ke6 7. a7 Kd6 { runs into } 8. Ke4 $1 { with a decisive zugzwang. } ) 
4. Kd5 $1 { In the same spirit of activity above material. } ( { In contrast, } 4. Rxe5 $2 Ra1 ( { or } 4. Kd5 Ra4 ) 
5. Rxc5 Ke6 { is a draw. } ) 
Ra7 5. Re6+ $1 { The e5-pawn is White's first priority. } ( { On } 5. Kxc5 $2 { Black can draw in several ways; e.g. } Re7 ( { or } 5. Re6+ Kf5 ) 
 ) 
Kf7 6. Rxe5 { Black's king has been fatally cut off. } Ra8 7. Re1 ( { But not } 7. Kxc5 $2 Ra5 $1 { when Black makes it into a drawn pawn ending, as observed by Ckmt98. } ) 
Rc8 8. Kd6 Kf8 9. Kd7 Ra8 10. Rf1+ $1 { Good technique, further displacing the enemy king. } Kg7 11. Kc6 Rc8+ 12. Kb7 Rd8 13. Rf5 Rd4 14. Rxc5 Kf6 15. Rc8 Ke6 { Black's king finally manages to get closer, but as we already know from Chapter Three, when the defender's king is placed on the long side of the pawn, salvation is not possible. } 16. c5 Kd5 17. c6 Rb4+ 18. Kc7 Rh4 19. Rg8 Ke6 20. Rd8 Ra4 21. Kb7 
 1-0

